      "narrative": "The copper lamps flicker as the morning rain drums against the shutters. Mara Venn looks up from her cup, her sharp eyes fixing on you with the weight of someone who's been waiting to be asked.\n\nShe sets the cup down and leans forward, fingers laced on the scarred tabletop.\n\n\"The Ash Gate,\" she says, voice low but clear. \"It's been sealed for almost a decade now. Not by rust or rot \u2014 by design. The lock was forged specifically to keep it that way, and Toran here was the one who maintained it.\" She gestures with a tilt of her head toward the nervous clerk at the next table.\n\nToran Vex flinches at the attention, ink-stained fingers fidgeting with a cuff.\n\n\"The gate leads to the Thornwood,\" Mara continues. \"Once, Bram ferried people through \u2014 traders, messengers, folk with business on the other side. But then the lock was... changed. Reinforced. Old Bram stayed on as keeper, but no one's passed through in years. Not officially.\"\n\nShe pauses, holding your gaze.\n\n\"The question everyone in Copper Finch wants answered is *why* the gate was sealed in the first place. Bram knew. He was there when it happened. But Bram's dead now, and the key \u2014 if there ever was one \u2014 went with him.\"\n\nShe lets that settle, then adds, softer: \"The gate's not broken, Kael. It was *locked*. On purpose. And someone went to a lot of trouble to make sure it stays that way.\""
    },
