      "narrative": "Mara Venn's fingers stop drumming. She holds your gaze for a long moment, then pulls something from an inner pocket of her charcoal coat \u2014 a folded scrap of paper, yellowed at the edges. She smooths it flat on the table between you.\n\nIt's a receipt. Faded ink, but legible. A name, a date, and a fee. Payment for passage through the Ash Gate, seven years ago.\n\nShe taps the name at the bottom with a fingernail.\n\n\"Old Bram,\" she says. \"He sold passage. Every time. He was the gatekeeper in truth \u2014 not just a ferryman. If you wanted through, you paid Bram, and he opened the lock. He kept the key on him at all times.\"\n\nShe lets you read the rest of the receipt: the name of a merchant who passed through, the toll of twelve silver, and Bram's spidery signature.\n\n\"But here's the part that matters.\" Her voice drops lower. \"The night before Bram died, he came to me. Drunk and shaking. Said someone had threatened him. Told him to stop letting people through \u2014 or else. He wouldn't tell me who. Just kept muttering about 'the name on the other side.'\"\n\nShe folds the receipt and tucks it away.\n\n\"Next morning, he was dead at the gate. Heart failure, the healer said. But I've seen enough death to know a man doesn't die of fright from a bad heart alone.\"\n\nShe leans back.\n\n\"The Sable Quill was the last person seen speaking to him alive. She might know the name Bram was too scared to say aloud.\""
    },
