      "narrative": "The door of the Copper Finch swings shut behind you, cutting off the warm lamplight and the murmur of voices. Rain slicks the cobblestones, a thin grey curtain that softens the edges of the town.\n\nYou cross the muddy square. The buildings thin out. The ground rises slightly, and then you see it.\n\nThe Ash Gate stands alone against the grey sky \u2014 a cracked black arch of stone, twice the height of a man, set into nothing. No wall flanks it. No road leads through it. Just the arch, standing in the wet grass like a wound in the world.\n\nA figure sits on a crate beside the gate, hooded and still. Travel-stained cloak, mud-caked boots. A courier's satchel rests at her feet.\n\nShe doesn't look up as you approach. Just watches the gate with the patience of someone who's been sitting there a long time.\n\nThe air around the arch is cold. Colder than the rain. You feel it on your skin as you step closer.\n\nThe Sable Quill.\n\nShe shifts, finally. A gloved hand reaches down, touches the satchel.\n\n\"You're the one from the Finch,\" she says. Voice flat. Tired. \"Mara sent you.\""
    },
